1
00:05:34,285 --> 00:05:40,285
The world is more giant than you can imagine.

2
00:11:08,571 --> 00:11:14,571
The BFG is a adventure, family, fantasy film directed by Steven Spielberg.

3
00:16:42,857 --> 00:16:48,857
An orphan little girl befriends a benevolent giant who takes her to Giant Country, where
they attempt to stop the man-eating giants that are invading the human world.

4
00:22:17,142 --> 00:22:23,142
The BFG, played by Mark Rylance.

5
00:27:51,428 --> 00:27:57,428
Sophie, played by Ruby Barnhill.

6
00:33:25,714 --> 00:33:31,714
Mary, played by Rebecca Hall.

7
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:06,000
Fleshlumpeater, played by Jemaine Clement.

8
00:44:34,285 --> 00:44:40,285
Bloodbottler, played by Bill Hader.

9
00:50:08,571 --> 00:50:14,571
The Queen, played by Penelope Wilton.

10
00:55:42,857 --> 00:55:48,857
The BFG (titled onscreen as Roald Dahl's The BFG "Big Friendly Giant") is a 2016 fantasy adventure film
directed by Steven Spielberg, written by Melissa Mathison, and based on the 1982 novel.

11
01:01:17,142 --> 01:01:23,142
The film stars Mark Rylance, Ruby Barnhill, Penelope Wilton, Jemaine
Clement, Rebecca Hall, Rafe Spall, and Bill Hader.

12
01:06:51,428 --> 01:06:57,428
In the film, a ten-year-old orphaned girl named Sophie befriends a
benevolent giant and helps him to deal with some man-eating giants.

13
01:12:25,714 --> 01:12:31,714
Kathleen Kennedy and Frank Marshall began development on a live-action adaptation of The BFG in the 1990s,
and various screenwriters were hired to work on the screenplay in the subsequent years.

14
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
DreamWorks acquired the screen rights to Dahl's book in September 2011,
and Spielberg was announced as director in April 2014.

15
01:23:34,285 --> 01:23:40,285
Principal photography commenced in March 2015.

16
01:29:08,571 --> 01:29:14,571
The BFG premiered at the Cannes Film Festival on May 14, 2016, and debuted
in North America at the El Capitan Theatre on June 21, 2016.

17
01:34:42,857 --> 01:34:48,857
The film was released in the United States on July 1,
2016, the same year of Dahl's centennial.

18
01:40:17,142 --> 01:40:23,142
It received generally positive reviews from critics, and was a box office
disappointment, grossing $195 million against its $140 million budget.

19
01:45:51,428 --> 01:45:57,428
It was the last film penned by Mathison before her
death in 2015, and was dedicated to her.

20
01:51:25,714 --> 01:51:31,714
The film explores themes of london, england,
england, queen, little girl, orphan.
